@LourdesGálvezdelPostigo And yet Malaga's mayor keeps being re-elected. I live to the East of Malaga (because when I was buying a decade ago I looked at the seemingly unplanned conurbation that stretches from Estepona to Rincon and was horrified) and am now watching developers screw up the coast this side of Malaga. I'm not against development but there seems to be no planning structure within the Province - it seems to be just get approval for an urbanisation with no thought of how to supply it with basic services, or it's impact on traffic etc. We've just had a drought where villages were literally not getting water from midnight to 8am but it rained a lot last Winter so the Velez de-salination plant appears to have slipped down the list of priorities - despite massive new developments on the West side of Torre del Mar. According to Sur 13,000 flats are being held up in the Province because the electric grid can't supply the demand - but no suggestion that all new develoments have solar power, batteries and water retention schemes. And additional public transport? I've seen nothing to suggest anything other than buses. I'd be thinking of running a tram / light rail from Maria Zembrano station in Malaga East along the promenade and then follow the coast - it could meet the failed Velez Malaga / Torre del Mar system. There used to be a railway from Zafarraya via Velez and Torre to Malaga Port but it was closed fifty or sixty years ago - I've no idea if they kept the right of way, and I've heard nothing about the government securing new rights of way.

But I think it's simplistic to suggest it's all about tourists - Malaga has squarely established itself as a Tech hub (the second stage of the Tech Park has started construction recently) and that has brought with it lots of well paying jobs that has fuelled the local property market - from what I've read people have been moving from all over Spain to work there. The problem is that the government seems happy to let developers build what is most profitable rather than what the city needs. The city is a victim of it's own success.

So blaming tourists is easy but not really dealing with the issue - which is your politicians are addicted to construction and tourism, which is why there is no problem (economic, environmental or social) that a Malaga politician feels cannot be solved by a new hotel. And expanding the airport? Where are the new passengers going to go?

@LourdesGálvezdelPostigo I am sympathetic. In the US reporters have found that most of the time half the luxury skyline flats in Manhattan are vacant while homelessness abounds. 
Earmarking taxation for housing subsidies for houses under à set of values (else prices rise). 100% is silly and would move investment. Taxes on forefinger housing should be progressive, earmarked, and calculated to benifit Spain. Note there are many details such as rental prices of foreign purchased homes, % of year occupied, move to wealth and away from income tax. Demand well audited returns on foreign investment. etc. 
